  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">High quality tomato seeds for international market</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Note: This project has been cancelled, because the applicant has not been able to acquire proper land, in combination with the social unrest in the country.This project aims to set up a 4 ha production company of high quality hybrid determinate tomato seeds for export, with a maximum production of 1,750 kg of high quality seeds per year and a first production of 400 kg per year of lower quality seeds from two outgrowers.Vegetable seed production is very labour intensive. Most production of tomato seeds takes place in China. The climate in the specific growing region allows only one growing cycle per year. Therefore, most of the leading vegetable breeders have moved their focus towards East Africa, in particular Kenya. Ethiopia possesses good growing opportunities for vegetable seeds. The climate around Ziway is very suitable to produce year round high quality tomato seeds. Currently, there are no producers of high quality hybrid tomato seeds for export purposes.Ammerlaan Roses B.V. and their local production company AQ Roses PLC were asked by Syngenta Seeds B.V. to start a production location of their hybrid determinate tomato seeds in the region of Ziway. A 4 ha production farm will be started to produce the seeds on an environmentally friendly way. Rain water will be collected, stored and disinfected for drip irrigation purposes of the tomato plants growing on substrate system. The latter allows re-using the drain water. The affluent water will be filtered through wetlands. In addition to the main production location, ten farmers will be trained as outgrowers on lower quality tomato seeds production.In the years after the project it is the intention that hundreds of outgrowers will start the production of hybrid tomato seeds. AQ Roses PLC is well known for its high social standards in Ethiopia, including Ethical Trade Initiative, certified by the NGO Africa Now.</narrative>
  </description>
